<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#monsoon">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monsoon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The monsoon is a seasonal reversal of winds that brings heavy summer rains to South and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Monsoon rains governed agriculture and seaborne trade across the region. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> suggest that prolonged droughts and intense monsoon variability may have strained the hydraulic system at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> and contributed to its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monsoon.jpg" alt="Monsoon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PlaneMad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monsoon_clouds_near_Nagercoil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1662 the Mughal governor of Bengal, Mir Jumla, invaded, occupied the capital Garhgaon and forced Jayadhwaj Singha to accept the harsh Treaty of Ghilajharighat in 1663, but disease and <b>monsoon</b> floods ruined the Mughal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivers and the <b>monsoon</b> were the best defence: invaders from Delhi in the 1350s found that they could not hold the country through the rains, and Ilyas Shah withstood Firuz Shah Tughluq by retreating into the fort of Ekdala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The vast hydraulic system that had sustained agricultural productivity became increasingly difficult to maintain, and tree-ring evidence points to prolonged droughts punctuated by intense <b>monsoons</b> in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries that may have damaged it further.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kilwa Kisiwani is a small island off the southern coast of present-day Tanzania, about 300 kilometres south of Dar es Salaam, with a sheltered harbour well placed for ships sailing on the <b>monsoon</b> winds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the northern caravan trade, had already reduced Sabaean dominance, and from about the second century BCE Greek and then Roman ships, sailing with the <b>monsoon</b> winds between Egypt and India, carried a growing share of incense and eastern goods by sea, bypassing the overland route past Marib.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from China, the Ryukyu Islands, Java and the Maluku Islands came in on the northeast <b>monsoon</b> from December to January, and ships for the Indian coasts, the Red Sea and East Africa left on the southwest monsoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
